4976
07:47:54,128 --> 07:47:59,728
If you have ever wondered how AI models are evaluated, this video will explain it in simple terms.

4977
07:47:59,968 --> 07:48:08,608
LLM benchmarks are used to check how good these models are at tasks like coding, answering questions, and translating languages or summarizing text.

4978
07:48:08,768 --> 07:48:13,567
These tests use sample data and specific measurement to see how will the model perform.

4979
07:48:13,888 --> 07:48:22,848
For example, the model might be tested with a few examples, like few short learning or none at all, like zero short learning, to see how it handles new tasks.

4980
07:48:23,008 --> 07:48:26,180
So now the question arises: why are these benchmarks important?

4981
07:48:26,542 --> 07:48:30,820
They help developers understand where a model is strong and where it needs improvement.

4982
07:48:31,118 --> 07:48:36,356
They also make it easier to compare different models, helping people choose the best one for their needs.

4983
07:48:36,528 --> 07:48:38,980
However, LLM benchmarks do have some limits.

4984
07:48:39,328 --> 07:48:43,012
They don't always predict how well a model will work in real-world situations.

4985
07:48:43,168 --> 07:48:49,284
And sometimes models can overfit, meaning they perform well on test data but struggle in practical use.

4986
07:48:49,582 --> 07:48:57,888
We will also cover how LLM leaderboards rank different model page on their benchmark scores, giving us a clear picture of which models are performing the best.

4987
07:48:58,158 --> 07:49:03,728
So stay tuned as we dive into how LLM benchmarks work and why they are so important for advancing AI.

4988
07:49:03,822 --> 07:49:05,648
So without any further ado, let's get started.

4989
07:49:05,870 --> 07:49:07,328
So, what are LLM benchmarks?

4990
07:49:07,534 --> 07:49:12,128
LLM benchmarks are standardized tools used to evaluate the performance of large language models.

4991
07:49:12,238 --> 07:49:19,728
They provide a structured way to test LLMs on a specific task or question using sample data and predefined metrics to measure their capabilities.

4992
07:49:19,982 --> 07:49:28,740
These benchmarks assess various skills such as coding, common sense reasoning, and NLP tasks like machine translation, question answering, and text summarization.

4993
07:49:28,878 --> 07:49:33,168
The importance of LLM benchmarks lies in their role in advancing model development.

4994
07:49:33,328 --> 07:49:40,768
They track the progress of an LLM, offering quantitative insights into where the model performs well and where improvement is needed.

4995
07:49:41,008 --> 07:49:47,648
This feedback is crucial for guiding the fine-tuning process, allowing researchers and developers to enhance model performance.

4996
07:49:47,808 --> 07:49:56,612
Additionally, benchmarks offers an objective comparison between different LLMs, helping developers and organizations choose the best model for their needs.

4997
07:49:56,848 --> 07:49:58,768
So, how LLM benchmarks work.

4998
07:49:58,990 --> 07:50:01,968
LLM benchmarks follow a clear and systematic process.

4999
07:50:02,158 --> 07:50:10,448
They present a task for LLM to complete, evaluate its performance using specific metrics, and assign a score based on how well the model performs.

5000
07:50:10,670 --> 07:50:13,648
So here is a breakdown of how this process works.

5001
07:50:13,902 --> 07:50:14,980
The first one is setup.

5002
07:50:15,246 --> 07:50:23,876
LLM benchmarks come with pre-prepared sample data including coding challenges, long documents, math problems, and real-world conversation.

5003
07:50:24,048 --> 07:50:34,288
The task span various areas like common sense reasoning, problem solving, question answering, summary generation, and translation, all present to the model at the start of testing.

5004
07:50:34,478 --> 07:50:36,128
The second step is testing.

5005
07:50:36,302 --> 07:50:38,448
The model is tested on one of the three ways.

5006
07:50:38,734 --> 07:50:46,448
Few short, the LLM is provided with a few examples before being prompted to complete a task, demonstrating its ability to learn from limited data.

5007
07:50:46,608 --> 07:50:48,068
The second one is zero shot.

5008
07:50:48,208 --> 07:50:56,208
The model is asked to perform a task without any prior examples, testing its ability to understand new concepts and adapt to unfamiliar scenarios.

5009
07:50:56,288 --> 07:50:57,540
The third one is fine-tuned.

5010
07:50:57,648 --> 07:51:05,408
The model is trained on a dataset similar to the one used in the benchmark, aiming to enhance its performance on the specific task involved.

5011
07:51:05,568 --> 07:51:07,044
The third step is scoring.

5012
07:51:07,328 --> 07:51:17,648
So after completing the task, the benchmark compares the model's output with the expected answer and generates a score, typically ranging from 0 to 100, reflecting how accurately the LLM performs.

5013
07:51:17,902 --> 07:51:19,248
So now let's move forward.

5014
07:51:19,568 --> 07:51:22,404
Let's see key metrics for benchmarking LLMs.

5015
07:51:22,734 --> 07:51:27,568
So LLM's benchmark uses various metrics to assess the performance of large language model.

5016
07:51:27,822 --> 07:51:29,728
So here are some commonly used metrics.

5017
07:51:30,048 --> 07:51:32,208
The first one is accuracy or precision.

5018
07:51:32,398 --> 07:51:35,328
Measure the percentage of correct prediction made by the model.

5019
07:51:35,760 --> 07:51:37,120
The second one is recall.

5020
07:51:37,326 --> 07:51:38,640
Also known as sensitivity.

5021
07:51:38,800 --> 07:51:43,280
Measure the number of true positives reflecting the correct prediction made by the model.

5022
07:51:43,502 --> 07:51:44,932
The third one is F1 score.

5023
07:51:45,120 --> 07:51:51,440
Combines both accuracy and recall into a single metric, weighing them equally to address any false positive or negatives.

5024
07:51:51,827 --> 07:51:56,240
F1 score ranging from 0 to 1, where 1 indicates perfect precision and recall.

5025
07:51:56,366 --> 07:51:57,924
The fourth one is exact match.

5026
07:51:58,254 --> 07:52:05,860
Tracks the percentage of predictions that exactly match the correct answer, which is especially used for the tasks like translation and question answering.

5027
07:52:06,160 --> 07:52:08,228
The fifth one is perplexity gauges.

5028
07:52:08,560 --> 07:52:13,120
Here it will tell you how well a model predicts the next word or token.

5029
07:52:13,230 --> 07:52:17,600
A lower perplexity score indicates better task comprehension by the model.

5030
07:52:17,806 --> 07:52:30,020
The sixth one is blue, bilingual evaluation Understudy, is used for evaluating machine translation by comparing Ngrams, sequence of adjacent text elements between the model's output and the human produced translation.

5031
07:52:30,400 --> 07:52:34,160
So these quantitative metrics are often combined for more through evaluation.

5032
07:52:34,318 --> 07:52:40,964
So in addition, human evaluation introduces qualitatively factors like coherence, relevance, and semantic meaning.

5033
07:52:41,280 --> 07:52:42,756
Provide a nuanced assessment.

5034
07:52:42,960 --> 07:52:51,520
However, human evaluation can be time-consuming and subjective, making a balance between quantitative and qualitative measures important for comprehensive evaluation.

5035
07:52:51,758 --> 07:52:55,280
So now let's moving forward and see some limitation of LLM benchmarking.

5036
07:52:55,374 --> 07:53:03,492
While LLM benchmarking are available for assessing model performance, they have several limitations that prevent them from fully predicting real-world effectiveness.

5037
07:53:03,726 --> 07:53:04,804
So here are some few.

5038
07:53:05,200 --> 07:53:07,012
The first one is bounded scoring.

5039
07:53:07,200 --> 07:53:16,772
Once a model achieves the highest possible scores on the benchmark, that benchmark loses its utility and must be updated with more challenging tasks to remain a meaningful assessment tool.

5040
07:53:16,960 --> 07:53:18,640
The second one is broad data set.

5041
07:53:18,894 --> 07:53:22,747
LLM benchmarks often rely on sample data from diverse subject and task.

5042
07:53:22,958 --> 07:53:32,000
So this wide scope may not effectively evaluate a model's performance in edge cases, specialized fields or specific use cases where more tailored data would be needed.

5043
07:53:32,238 --> 07:53:33,760
The third one is finite assessment.

5044
07:53:34,030 --> 07:53:43,588
Benchmark only tests a model current skills, and as LLMs evolve and new capabilities emerge, new benchmarks must be created to measure these advancements.

5045
07:53:43,758 --> 07:53:45,040
The fourth one is overfitting.

5046
07:53:45,166 --> 07:53:54,244
So if an LLM is trained on the same data used for benchmarking, it can be lead to overfitting where the model performs well on the test data but struggles with the real task.

5047
07:53:54,560 --> 07:53:59,076
So this result is scores that don't truly represent the model's broader capabilities.

5048
